\par\par \b \uc2 Press Release: Six in Ten UK Consumers Would Stop Using an AI Shopping Agent After One Mistake, \uc2 ACI\uc2  Survey Finds\b0\par\par\uc2 892 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2026\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2026, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Trust remains the critical barrier to adoption of AI-driven shopping tools, as UK consumer survey reveals fears over control, money and accountability \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par LONDON--(BUSINESS WIRE)--June 29, 2026-- \f28 \par \par \uc2 New research from \uc2 ACI Worldwide\uc2  (NASDAQ: ACIW), an original innovator in global payments technology, reveals a significant trust gap between \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  and human decision-makers, highlighting a key barrier to the widespread adoption of AI shopping agents.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 YouGov\uc2  survey of more than 2,000 UK adults, conducted on behalf of \uc2 ACI Worldwide\uc2 , found that just 19% of consumers trust AI assistants to follow rules to set up make every day purchasing decisions, compared with 55% who trust a human expert or adviser. Seven in ten (69%) do not trust AI, even when it follows rules they set, while six in ten (60%) say they would stop using an AI agent after just one mistake.\par \par \uc2 AI shopping agents are tools that can search, compare and, with a consumer's permission, complete purchases on their behalf. The findings come as merchants, payment providers and technology platforms increasingly move these tools beyond product discovery and closer to checkout and payment.\par \par \uc2 "The findings clearly show that consumers are open to AI helping them shop smarter, but only if they remain firmly in control of both the decision--making and their money," said Adriana Iordan, head of merchant and payments intelligence at \uc2 ACI Worldwide\uc2 . "They're telling us very clearly that they won't hand control of their finances to an autonomous agent without safeguards. This isn't a capability gap; it's a trust and confidence gap. If the industry wants adoption, it must prioritise control over capability: explicit approvals, hard spending limits, protected payment details and clear accountability when things go wrong."\par \par \uc2 Key Findings: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --  AI is acceptable as a tool, but not a decision--maker. While consumers \par       show some openness to AI as a support tool, trust collapses when AI is \par       given greater autonomy. This sharp drop-off highlights a clear boundary; \par       consumers are comfortable with AI assisting decisions, but deeply \par       uncomfortable with AI making them. \par  \par           --  50% of respondents trust AI to find the best price available, \par              with 43% trusting it to follow spending limits \par  \par           --  Only 18% trust AI to act in their best financial interest; just \par              17% trust it to keep personal and payment data secure and only 15% \par              trust AI to handle problems when something goes wrong \par  \par  \par  \par    --  Financial incentives fail to overcome fear. The research also shows \par       that savings alone are not enough to win consumers over. Resistance is \par       driven less by value and more by perceived risk and loss of control. \par  \par           --  44% say they would not trust an AI shopping agent regardless of \par              savings, and one in four say it would need to save them more than \par              15% before they would trust it \par  \par  \par  \par    --  Autonomy triggers strongest resistance. Concerns intensify when AI \par       agents act independently or access sensitive financial data; these fears \par       point to a fundamental discomfort with unsupervised, agent--led \par       commerce. \par  \par           --  Seven in ten (70%) say purchases made without asking would \par              affect their willingness to use an AI shopping agent; 61% say \par              linking it to a bank account, and 54% say tracking everything they \par              browse online would impact their willingness to use an AI agent \par  \par  \par  \par    --  When things go wrong, consumers don't hesitate to assign blame. The \par       majority of UK respondents believe responsibility falls squarely on AI \par       providers, a challenge many emerging commerce models have yet to \par       address. \par  \par           --  54% say the technology or AI company that built the AI agent \par              should be accountable for refunds \par  \par           --  Just 9% blame the retailer; only 3% blame banks or card issuers \par  \par  \par  \par  \par    --  No organisation inherits trust by default. Perhaps most strikingly, 59% \par       of consumers say they would not trust any organisation to manage \par       AI--powered shopping and payments. Even banks and other regulated \par       financial payments providers are chosen by just 20%, while technology \par       companies and retailers trail far behind at 4% each. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Note to editors: All figures, unless otherwise stated, are from \f28 \uc2 \f2 YouGov Plc.\f28 \uc2 \f2  Total sample size: 2,080 UK adults (18+). Fieldwork conducted between 19--22 June 2026. The survey was carried out online and weighted to be representative of the UK adult population.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 About \f28 \uc2 \f2 ACI Worldwide\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ACI Worldwide\f28 \uc2 \f2 , an original innovator in global payments technology, delivers transformative software solutions that power intelligent payments orchestration in real time so banks, billers and merchants can drive growth, while continuously modernizing their payment infrastructures, simply and securely. With nearly 50 years of trusted payments expertise, we combine our global footprint with a local presence to offer enhanced payment experiences to stay ahead of constantly changing payment challenges and opportunities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copyright ACI Worldwide, Inc. 2026\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ACI, ACI Worldwide, ACI Payments, Inc., ACI Pay, Speedpay and all ACI product/solution names are trademarks or registered trademarks of ACI Worldwide, Inc., or one of its subsidiaries, in the United States, other countries or both. Other parties' trademarks referenced are the property of their respective owners.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 View source version on businesswire.com: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260628382944/en/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260628382944/en/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     CONTACT: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Media Contacts\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Katrin Boettger | Communications and Corporate Affairs Director | katrin.boettger@aciworldwide.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pierce Rohrmann | Head of Communications and Corporate Affairs | pierce.rohrmann@aciworldwide.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 29, 2026 01:00 ET (05:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Document DJDN000020260629em6t000ei\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
