\par\par \b \uc2 Part of \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  Model Ban Is Rolled Back -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 683 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2026\par 08:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2026, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 U.S. allows trusted partners to access Mythos 5 version; Fable 5 limits kept\par \par \uc2 By Amrith Ramkumar\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The Trump administration is allowing \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  to reoffer one of its banned AI models to trusted companies and government partners, a key step toward rolling back restrictions that fueled industry concern about ad hoc federal regulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  can allow dozens of companies and partners trusted by the government to access Mythos 5, one of the two models that the administration banned for foreign use two weeks ago, Commerce Secretary \uc2 Howard Lutnick\uc2  said in a Friday letter to \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  Chief Compute Officer Tom Brown, a copy of which was viewed by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Fable 5, a general-purpose version of \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 's powerful Mythos model that was also banned, remains restricted, and restrictions on Mythos 5 still apply to entities that aren't trusted partners.\par \par \uc2 The olive branch from the administration is the result of two weeks of talks led by Lutnick and Brown to address the government's security concerns, which were sparked by \uc2 Amazon\uc2  researchers who found a way to evade Fable's safeguards.\par \par \uc2 "These efforts have yielded significant progress. In addition, \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  has committed to work with the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  on protocols and standards and releases for the covered models," Lutnick said in the letter.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  spokesman said the company was working to restore access to Mythos 5 for the partners as soon as possible. "We are pleased to see this progress and continue to work with the government to expand access to Mythos 5 and make Fable 5 available for general use again," he said.\par \par \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  had previously said that it felt it had adequate safeguards in place and didn't think the issue raised by the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  researchers was as serious as some cybersecurity experts had claimed. The company has feuded with the Trump administration for months over AI usage in the military and \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 's past ties to nonprofits that are big donors to liberal causes.\par \par \uc2 President Trump recently praised \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Dario Amodei\uc2 's response to the model ban.\par \par \uc2 "He responded very responsibly," Trump told \uc2 Axios\uc2 , adding that Amodei seemed "nice" and "smart" when the pair took part in a \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  AI meeting in France while negotiations were ongoing. Earlier this year, Trump called \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  a "radical-left, woke company" when the Pentagon designated the company a security risk.\par \par \uc2 Semafor previously reported on the administration's decision.\par \par \uc2 The Commerce Department had restricted all usage of Fable 5 and Mythos 5 by foreign individuals or entities, prompting \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  to shut down all access to comply with the unprecedented rule. It marked the government's biggest intervention in the rapidly evolving AI industry.\par \par \uc2 Mythos 5 was restricted to trusted partners before the government ban. Mythos Preview, an earlier version of Mythos that had been gradually made available to companies and governments managing critical infrastructure, has remained an option to users, but the Trump administration's concerns about model safety have prompted concerns across the industry.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  said it was delaying the public rollout of its latest models, known as GPT-5.6, because of government security concerns. The company is letting trusted partners access it with government approval and hopes to expand access. Like Mythos, the models have demonstrated the ability to efficiently find software bugs, capabilities that could be used in cyberattacks.\par \par \uc2 The White House is racing to implement an executive order that was recently signed giving federal cybersecurity officials more of a say in AI model evaluation. Until the order is implemented and standards are in place, companies are in limbo and reacting to case-by-case decisions from the administration, executives have said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Amrith Ramkumar at amrith.ramkumar@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (June 27, 2026).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 27, 2026 02:33 ET (06:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Document DJDN000020260627em6r00042\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
