\par\par \b \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  Limits Rollout of Advanced Models. Blame the Feds. -- Barrons.com\b0\par\par\uc2 By Angela Palumbo \par \uc2 474 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2026\par 21:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2026, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  on Friday said it would limit the release of its newest models at the request of the Trump administration, the latest example of federal scrutiny of the \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  industry.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  said it would begin a limited preview of the GPT-5.6 series, which includes three models: Sol, Terra, and Luna.\par \par \uc2 The company said it plans to make the new models generally available in the coming weeks. However, at the request of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  is starting with a limited preview of the models for a small group of partners whose participation has been shared with the government.\par \par \uc2 "We don't believe this kind of government access process should become the long-term default. It keeps the best tools from users, developers, enterprises, cyber defenders, and global partners who need them," \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  said in the blog post.\par \par \uc2 It's not the first time an AI company has slowly rolled out a new model to prevent cybersecurity risks, nor is it the first time President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's administration has gotten involved in the release of these models.\par \par \uc2 Fellow AI firm \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  didn't make it's most capable model, Mythos, available to the public due to concerns that security firms may not be able to handle the cyber threats it poses. A select number of companies were given access to Claude Mythos Preview in a partnership called Project Glasswing, which was announced on April 7.\par \par \uc2 On June 2, the White House issued an executive order establishing a voluntary process for AI models to be assessed for cybersecurity threats.\par \par \uc2 On June 9, \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  released Claude Fable 5, a "Mythos-class" model, for general use. Then on June 12, \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  suspended access to Fable 5 after the government issued an export control directive to suspend access to those models by any foreign national, citing national security concerns.\par \par \uc2 "As we have stated publicly, we believe the government should have the ability to block unsafe deployments, as part of a statutory process that is transparent, fair, clear, and grounded in technical facts. This action does not adhere to those principles," \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  said in a June 12 blog post.\par \par \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  staffers reportedly met with Trump administration officials last week to try to resolve the dispute between the firm and the government.\par \par \uc2 When asked if Trump views \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  as a threat to national security in an \uc2 Axios\uc2  interview on June 19, the president said, "well, not now, but a week ago, maybe."\par \par \uc2 Write to Angela Palumbo at angela.palumbo@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 This content was created by Barron's, which is operated by Dow Jones & Co. Barron's is published independently from Dow Jones Newswires and The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 26, 2026 15:29 ET (19:29 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Document DJDN000020260626em6q0030f\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary: AI\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 Artificial intelligence\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbr